WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.130
You help an elderly person cross the street, and then, almost without meaning to, your eyes drift around to check whether anyone saw you.

2
00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:12.420
Did that glance just wipe out the kindness you'd done a moment before?

3
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:21.190
A thousand years ago, a Sufi from Baghdad was so troubled by this question that he wrote an entire book on how a person can test their own intentions.

4
00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:32.720
A muddy country road in Illinois.

5
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.730
An old stagecoach jolts along.

6
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:43.860
Inside sits Abraham Lincoln, years before anyone had even spoken of him as a future president, along with a traveling companion.

7
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.390
The two are deep in debate.

8
00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:53.080
Lincoln is arguing that beneath every good deed a person does, there lies, in the end, a selfish motive.

9
00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.730
His companion objects, but Lincoln won't budge.

10
00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:01.340
Just then, as they pass a creek, they hear a sharp animal cry.

11
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:08.030
On the bank, a mother pig is thrashing and squealing; her piglets have sunk into the mire and are about to drown.

12
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:15.680
Lincoln tells the driver to stop, climbs down, pulls the piglets out of the mud one by one, and sets them on dry ground.

13
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:23.170
When he climbs back into the coach, covered in mud, his companion smiles and asks him where the selfishness was in all that.

14
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:26.660
Lincoln's reply is the kind that ends an argument.

15
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:31.110
That was the very essence of selfishness, he says.

16
00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:37.640
I would have had no peace of mind all day if I had gone on and left that suffering old sow worrying over those pigs.

17
00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.970
I did it to get peace of mind for myself.

18
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.140
The story was told in an Illinois newspaper.

19
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.950
We don't know for certain whether it really happened this way.

20
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.440
Even so, philosophers fell in love with it.

21
00:01:51.440 --> 00:02:01.170
The American philosopher Joel Feinberg uses this scene as an example in his essay on psychological egoism, a piece that has been taught in universities for generations.

22
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:09.780
Psychological egoism is the view that people act only in their own interest, and that what we call kindness is really personal satisfaction in disguise.

23
00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.910
Lincoln's answer is the most charming defense this view has ever had.

24
00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:23.800
And yet the answer has a flaw, and that flaw was spotted more than a hundred years before Lincoln, at the start of the eighteenth century, by the English clergyman Joseph Butler.

25
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.810
Feinberg follows in his footsteps.

26
00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:33.340
The question is this: why would Lincoln have felt uneasy if he had left those piglets behind?

27
00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:37.630
Only because he genuinely wanted them to be saved.

28
00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:45.600
There must first be a desire that cares about someone else's good, so that when that desire is fulfilled, a person can feel at ease.

29
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.210
You don't feel pangs of conscience over something you don't care about at all.

30
00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:54.820
So peace of mind is not the goal of kindness, but its shadow.

31
00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.270
It follows the one who does good, but it doesn't steer them.

32
00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:04.520
Someone whose only aim was their own peace of mind would never have stepped out of that coach.

33
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:08.970
They would have shut their eyes, covered their ears, and gone on their way.

34
00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:15.180
But hidden inside the Lincoln story is a subtler and more unsettling question.

35
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.710
There was a witness in that scene.

36
00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:23.600
Lincoln had just claimed that all good deeds are selfish, and someone was sitting right beside him, watching.

37
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.730
Had he been alone in the coach, would he still have climbed down?

38
00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:32.340
The oldest form of this question in philosophy appears in Plato's Republic.

39
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.110
Glaucon, one of Socrates' partners in debate, tells a story.

40
00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:42.200
A shepherd named Gyges finds a ring in a chasm that opens up after an earthquake.

41
00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:47.290
When he turns the ring's stone toward the palm of his hand, he becomes invisible.

42
00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:55.100
Once the shepherd discovers this power, he goes to the palace, seduces the queen, kills the king, and seizes the throne.

43
00:03:55.100 --> 00:04:02.590
Glaucon's claim is plain: put such a ring on the finger of the most honest person alive, and they would do exactly the same.

44
00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:06.880
What we call justice is just a decorated name for the fear of getting caught.

45
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:11.330
People behave well not because they are good, but because they are seen.

46
00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:15.140
You don't need a ring from a fairy tale to test this claim.

47
00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:25.670
The restaurant you eat at in a city you'll never visit again, the shopping cart left unreturned in a parking lot where no one is looking, a wallet found on the sidewalk with no identification inside...

48
00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:30.440
All of these are the little Gyges rings of everyday life.

49
00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.250
So there are two separate questions in front of us.

50
00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:39.180
First: when we do good, are we also gaining something for ourselves?

51
00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.670
Butler's answer took some of the edge off that question.

52
00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:46.800
The second is far tougher: does our goodness need an audience?

53
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:55.890
In the eighteenth century, this second question landed on the desks of two thinkers who lived hundreds of kilometers apart, and it reached each of them in a different form.

54
00:04:55.890 --> 00:05:05.300
In seventeen eighty-five, in Königsberg, Immanuel Kant published a short but enormously influential book, the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals.

55
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:08.150
In it there is a simple shop scene.

56
00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:13.400
A merchant does not overcharge customers who know nothing about buying and selling.

57
00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:18.970
If a child walks into his shop, the child pays the same price as everyone else.

58
00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:21.500
His conduct is flawless.

59
00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:26.750
Kant, however, asks a question: why does the merchant do this?

60
00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:38.400
If he does it because he has calculated that honesty is good business policy, that a shop known for honesty wins customers, then his action is in accordance with duty, but it does not spring from duty.

61
00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.890
He has done the right thing, but for the wrong reason.

62
00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.060
Kant takes an even bolder step.

63
00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:51.270
Some people are helpful by nature; they take a genuine, inner pleasure in bringing joy to others.

64
00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:59.240
According to Kant, their conduct is lovable, and it deserves praise and encouragement, but it does not deserve moral esteem.

65
00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.530
Their kindness comes from an inclination, not from a principle.

66
00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:07.180
Then he asks the reader to imagine someone else.

67
00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:13.550
This person's own grief has extinguished all his compassion, and the troubles of others no longer move him at all.

68
00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:18.640
Even so, with no desire to help, he helps simply because it ought to be done.

69
00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.410
For Kant, this is where true moral worth first appears.

70
00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.420
Let's carry this idea into the present day.

71
00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:30.710
It's the middle of the night, and you're coming home completely exhausted.

72
00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:35.480
Your neighbor's elderly mother is stuck in front of the building with heavy shopping bags.

73
00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:39.610
You don't feel like helping at all, and nobody is watching.

74
00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:44.380
Still, you pick up the bags and carry them up four flights of stairs.

75
00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:51.070
On Kant's scales, this act weighs more than the same help given on a cheerful day in front of a group of friends.

76
00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:59.360
Perhaps Kant's most honest lines are these: we can never be fully certain that any action was truly done from a sense of duty.

77
00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.770
Even the most rigorous self-examination cannot reach the hidden springs of our actions.

78
00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:07.300
Kant calls this the dear self.

79
00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:13.190
Even behind the decision we believe to be our noblest, that dear self may be lying in wait.

80
00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:15.880
So Kant offers us no comfort.

81
00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:22.090
If worth lies in an inner principle that no one can see, then even we cannot fully see that principle.

82
00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:29.580
Twenty-six years before Kant, in seventeen fifty-nine, Adam Smith, who was lecturing on moral philosophy in Glasgow,

83
00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:35.470
had approached the question from a completely different direction in his book The Theory of Moral Sentiments.

84
00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:41.840
Today we know him mostly as an economist, but for Smith, we first learn morality through the eyes of others.

85
00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:51.250
Someone who grew up in a solitary place without ever seeing a human face could no more know whether his conduct was good or bad than whether his own face was handsome or ugly.

86
00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:53.780
Society is our mirror.

87
00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:59.990
Over time we take these outside gazes into ourselves, and a spectator forms within our breast.

88
00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:06.520
Smith calls this spectator the impartial spectator, the man within the breast, the great judge of our conduct.

89
00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:11.130
This is where Smith's most valuable distinction for our subject begins.

90
00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:16.860
A person can love two different things: being praised, and being worthy of praise.

91
00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:23.390
A mature person takes no pleasure in praise he has not earned; he may even feel ashamed of it.

92
00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:30.080
On the other hand, if he has done something praiseworthy, his conscience is at peace even if no one notices.

93
00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:39.010
To the question, would you still do it if no one saw, Smith's answer is this: someone always sees, and that someone is the spectator within.

94
00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:45.060
The real question is whether that spectator is truly impartial, or merely an echo of the crowd outside.

95
00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:48.550
So how powerful is that outside crowd?

96
00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:55.080
In two thousand six, psychologists at Newcastle University in England ran a simple experiment.

97
00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:59.690
The department's tea room had an honesty box that no one supervised.

98
00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:04.300
Anyone who took tea or coffee dropped the money into the box themselves.

99
00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:10.510
The researchers placed a small image next to the price list above the box, changing it from week to week.

100
00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:16.560
Some weeks it was a photograph of a pair of eyes, other weeks a picture of flowers.

101
00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:23.090
In the weeks with eyes, the money put into the box rose to nearly three times what it was in the weeks with flowers.

102
00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:25.620
No one was actually watching.

103
00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:29.590
There was only a pair of eyes printed on paper on the wall.

104
00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:35.160
Smith's hope was that the spectator within us would mature enough not to need those paper eyes.

105
00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.610
The experiment shows that the spectator is still fed from outside.

106
00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:42.780
Kant and Smith were rightly troubled by this.

107
00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:50.750
But roughly nine hundred years before them, in Baghdad, a tradition had arisen that carried the same concern to a far darker depth.

108
00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:55.520
What concerned this tradition was not only whether people did good for an audience.

109
00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:01.250
What they really wanted to know was how the desire to be seen hides itself even from the one who holds it.

110
00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:05.380
The word riya comes from an Arabic root meaning to see.

111
00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:12.550
The word itself carries the question at the start of this episode: doing something to be seen, doing something to show.

112
00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:21.160
In Islamic thought, the subtlest and most unsettling analysis of riya was made by al-Harith al-Muhasibi, who lived in Baghdad in the ninth century.

113
00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:27.210
His name comes from the word for accounting, because he was a man who ceaselessly called himself to account.

114
00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:30.700
He died in Baghdad in eight fifty-seven.

115
00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:36.910
Junayd of Baghdad, the great Sufi of the following generations, was among those in his circle.

116
00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:43.920
Muhasibi treats riya not as a moral flaw but as a disease of the heart, and he examines it with a physician's care.

117
00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:49.810
In his book on observing the rights of God, he calls on his reader to look inside his own mind.

118
00:10:49.810 --> 00:10:58.900
He examines, one by one, the first stirring of intention, the thought that surfaces in the middle of a deed, and the residue that lingers in the heart after the deed is done.

119
00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.590
Riya can seep in through any of them.

120
00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:04.600
One person begins with a pure intention.

121
00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:12.090
In the middle of prayer, someone enters the room, and the recitation lengthens on its own, the bows grow slower and heavier.

122
00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:20.220
Another has never told anyone about a kindness he did, but when the conversation somehow turns that way, the tone of his voice changes.

123
00:11:20.220 --> 00:11:24.190
Muhasibi's concern is not the two-faced impostors.

124
00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:26.720
They are obvious enough.

125
00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:31.330
What truly interests him is the good person who believes himself sincere.

126
00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:34.980
This analysis rests on a short but startling hadith.

127
00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:46.150
According to the Musnad of Ahmad ibn Hanbal, the Prophet said that what he feared most for his community was the lesser idolatry, and when he was asked what that was, he answered: showing off.

128
00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:50.120
This is why showing off came to be called hidden idolatry.

129
00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:59.370
The reasoning goes like this: if a deed is said to be done for God, but you quietly keep an eye on how people see you, you have given worship a partner.

130
00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:04.140
Another narration describes this hiddenness with an image that is hard to forget.

131
00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:11.310
Idolatry within this community is more hidden than the footsteps of a black ant crawling across a black stone on a dark night.

132
00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:15.280
A dark night, a black stone, a black ant.

133
00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:19.890
The eye cannot see it and the ear cannot hear it, yet the ant keeps walking.

134
00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:29.300
The man who took up al-Muhasibi's hunt for this ant most fully, more than two centuries later, was al-Ghazali, who died in Tus in the year eleven eleven.

135
00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:38.230
His masterpiece, the Ihya Ulum al-Din, The Revival of the Religious Sciences, contains books devoted to the diseases of the heart.

136
00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:42.840
One of them directly condemns the love of fame and the habit of showing off.

137
00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:47.770
Here al-Ghazali makes no secret of how much he owes to al-Muhasibi.

138
00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:53.180
When he lists the showcases of showing off, he writes almost like a sociologist.

139
00:12:53.180 --> 00:13:01.310
A person can show off with the body, taking care to look pale and worn so that others will guess he spends his nights in prayer or his days fasting.

140
00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:07.680
He can show off with his clothing, wearing coarse, patched garments that announce he has turned his back on the world.

141
00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:15.010
He can show off with his words, sprinkling wise sayings through the conversation and letting everyone see how much he has memorized.

142
00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:18.980
He can show off with his deeds, and this is the best-known kind.

143
00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:26.950
And he can show off with his circle, forever talking about which scholars he has met, whose door he has visited, and who has come to visit him.

144
00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:32.680
What makes al-Ghazali truly unsettling is the way he breaks showing off into layers.

145
00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:39.370
In its crudest form, a person does something only to be seen, and would never do it at all if no one were there.

146
00:13:39.370 --> 00:13:47.020
In a subtler form, the deed is still done for God, but when someone is watching it becomes easier, lighter, sweeter.

147
00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:52.110
In the subtlest form of all, the audience has no effect on the deed whatsoever.

148
00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:56.400
The person does good alone and tells no one.

149
00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:01.170
But when the good deed comes to light one day, the heart stirs with joy.

150
00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:04.020
He expects a little respect from people.

151
00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:13.910
When others fail to greet him first, when his work is not received with enthusiasm, when he is not given special consideration at the market, he feels a pang inside.

152
00:14:13.910 --> 00:14:19.960
In al-Ghazali's diagnosis, that pang gives the heart away: it was expecting something in return.

153
00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:24.090
The hidden good deed had been entered into a secret ledger of debts owed.

154
00:14:24.090 --> 00:14:30.620
To put it in today's terms, picture someone who has volunteered at a hospital for years without ever telling a soul.

155
00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:37.150
One day the hospital management holds a ceremony to thank its volunteers, and forgets that person's name.

156
00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:41.600
The small hurt they feel is al-Ghazali's ant on the black stone.

157
00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:50.690
Or think of someone who gives anonymously, yet in the middle of a conversation, almost by accident, mentions what a beautiful feeling it is to donate anonymously.

158
00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:56.580
Al-Ghazali does not close off every way out, but he draws his distinctions carefully.

159
00:14:56.580 --> 00:15:01.510
He says that being glad when a good deed becomes known is not always a fault.

160
00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:11.560
A person may rejoice out of gratitude that God has revealed what is beautiful in him and covered what is ugly, or rejoice in the hope that others will be inspired to do the same good.

161
00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:14.410
What matters is the source of the joy.

162
00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:21.740
What is more, al-Ghazali sees giving up good deeds altogether out of fear of showing off as a trap in its own right.

163
00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:30.830
One of the devil's most cunning tricks is to whisper, this would just be showing off for you, and so keep a person from charity, from worship, from helping others.

164
00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:41.360
This bind is captured most sharply in a saying attributed to Fudayl ibn Iyad, a Sufi who lived even before al-Muhasibi: Abandoning a deed because of people is showing off.

165
00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:45.010
Doing a deed for the sake of people is idolatry.

166
00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:48.660
Sincerity is when God saves you from both.

167
00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:53.910
In other words, showing is a trap, and so is falling silent for fear of showing.

168
00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:58.040
These words were spoken more than a thousand years ago.

169
00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:03.930
But they never weighed as heavily as they do today, in a world where everyone carries a camera in their pocket.

170
00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:11.900
In a hadith reported by al-Bukhari and Muslim, seven people are named who will be sheltered in God's shade on a day when there is no other shade.

171
00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:18.270
One of them is the person who gives charity so secretly that the left hand does not know what the right hand has given.

172
00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:21.600
The same image appears in another tradition as well.

173
00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:33.570
In the Gospel of Matthew, in the Sermon on the Mount, Jesus tells those who give not to sound a trumpet before them as the hypocrites do in the streets, and asks that the left hand not know what the right hand is doing.

174
00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:40.420
Two great traditions, each on its own, turn to the same body and the same two hands.

175
00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:44.550
This suggests the question is one that all of humanity shares.

176
00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:48.040
The Quran, for its part, strikes a balance.

177
00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:57.290
According to the two hundred and seventy-first verse of Surah al-Baqarah, giving charity openly is good, but hiding it and giving it to those in need is better still.

178
00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:01.580
The Quran forbids neither path, but it holds one above the other.

179
00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:07.950
And that preference concerns not only the heart of the giver, but also the dignity of the one who receives.

180
00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:16.560
In Ottoman cities, in mosque courtyards or along the roadside, you could find stone pillars about the height of a person, hollowed out at the top.

181
00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:19.410
These were called charity stones.

182
00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:24.820
The giver usually came after dark, left their money in the hollow, and walked away.

183
00:17:24.820 --> 00:17:29.110
Later, the one in need would come and take only as much as they needed.

184
00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:33.720
The giver never saw the receiver, and the receiver never saw the giver.

185
00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:38.970
No one became indebted to anyone, and no one was diminished in anyone's eyes.

186
00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:49.980
In the twelfth century, the Jewish thinker Maimonides, who lived in Cairo, ranked charity on eight levels, and he placed giving in which the giver and the receiver do not know each other just below the highest.

187
00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:56.510
At the very top, he placed the kind of help that lifts a person to their feet so that they never need help again.

188
00:17:56.510 --> 00:18:05.440
Different faiths and different cities meet in the same intuition: secrecy protects the heart of the giver, but it also protects the dignity of the one who receives.

189
00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:10.210
Today we live in a world that is the exact opposite of the charity stone.

190
00:18:10.210 --> 00:18:14.980
A food parcel is handed over as the door opens, facing the camera.

191
00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:21.350
A pair of shoes given to a street child travels to millions of screens along with the child's astonished face.

192
00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:25.640
The help is real: the bread is eaten, the shoes are worn.

193
00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:29.130
But something has also been taken from that child.

194
00:18:29.130 --> 00:18:35.660
Their need has been placed in someone else's showcase of kindness, without anyone asking for their consent.

195
00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:40.110
What the charity stone protected for centuries is gone in a single shot.

196
00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:44.880
Still, we have to be honest here, because the matter is not black and white.

197
00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:50.770
A donation campaign launched for a sick child could never have reached its goal if it had not been shared.

198
00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:54.900
A friend's photo of giving blood might send you to the hospital too.

199
00:18:54.900 --> 00:19:03.990
It is no accident that al-Ghazali left the door ajar for good deeds made public in order to set an example, or that the Quran also counts open giving as good.

200
00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:12.280
Here, Fudayl's dilemma returns with all its weight: if you share, it may be showing off; if you don't, perhaps no one will act at all.

201
00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:18.650
What is truly new is that this dilemma is no longer an exceptional moment but has become everyday life itself.

202
00:19:18.650 --> 00:19:25.340
Glaucon's ring made a person invisible, and as a way of testing morality, that was an imaginary exception.

203
00:19:25.340 --> 00:19:29.630
The devices in our pockets have turned that ring inside out.

204
00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:32.480
We are almost never invisible.

205
00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:38.690
Every action is a potential stage, every kindness a potential piece of content.

206
00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:43.300
On the wall of the tea room in Newcastle there was a pair of paper eyes.

207
00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:49.830
On our screens, there are eyes that can be counted, that accumulate, that come back to us as notifications.

208
00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:57.960
Adam Smith's two loves, the love of praise and the love of being praiseworthy, are now the two things hardest to tell apart.

209
00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:04.970
Because praise arrives instantly and in numbers, while the quiet peace of being worthy always lags a step behind.

210
00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:08.620
What Kant called the dear self now has a counter of its own.

211
00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:13.870
Perhaps that is why sincerity should be thought of not as a test, but as a practice.

212
00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:18.160
If Kant is right, we will never see every fold of our hearts.

213
00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:22.610
If al-Ghazali is right, the ant may be walking at any moment.

214
00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:31.060
Then what we can do is not try to prove our purity, but train the spectator within us to live independently of outside eyes.

215
00:20:31.060 --> 00:20:36.630
And the way to do that runs through small kindnesses that no one will ever know about.

216
00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:42.520
In many neighborhoods across Turkey, the tradition of suspended bread still lives on in bakeries.

217
00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:47.930
Someone pays for two loaves, takes one, and the other is left hanging on the hook.

218
00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:50.780
No one knows who left it or who took it.

219
00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:54.110
There is no photograph, and no thank-you.

220
00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:59.200
It is the charity stone's small grandchild, on the scale of a neighborhood.

221
00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:09.890
The honest answer to the question "Would you still do it if no one saw?" will probably be, for most of us, "I don't know." But this question is not a verdict; it is a mirror.

222
00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:15.300
Whenever it comes to mind, it helps us examine our kindness of that moment once again.

223
00:21:15.300 --> 00:21:20.230
Perhaps sincerity is a place we never reach, but turn toward anew every day.

224
00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:24.520
The person who takes the suspended bread will never learn who left it.

225
00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:28.010
And the one who left it will never know where the bread went.

226
00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:31.340
There may be a little loneliness in this not knowing.

227
00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:36.910
But within that loneliness, kindness can, for the first time, remain simply kindness.

228
00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:41.200
We have reached the end of today's episode of Nöron Philosophy.

229
00:21:41.200 --> 00:21:43.730
Until we meet again in a new story.
